Input: {"question": "Who was Jingzong's father?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d00", "start": 0, "end": 150, "text": "Emperor Jingzong of Tang Emperor Jingzong of Tang (July 22, 809 – January 9, 827), personal name Li Zhan, was an emperor of the Tang Dynasty of China."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d00", "start": 151, "end": 178, "text": "He reigned from 824 to 827."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d00", "start": 179, "end": 306, "text": "Emperor Jingzong was the eldest son of emperor Emperor Muzong and elder brother of eventual Emperor Wenzong and Emperor Wuzong."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d00", "start": 307, "end": 564, "text": "Emperor Jingzong became emperor at the early age of 15 and his short reign would be overshadowed by corrupt eunuchs with control over the imperial Shence Army (神策軍), who would come to dominate his rule as well as that of his younger brother Emperor Wenzong."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d00", "start": 565, "end": 577, "text": "Uninterested"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d01", "start": 0, "end": 13, "text": ", 240 , 241 ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d01", "start": 14, "end": 459, "text": "Regnal titles Preceded by Emperor Shunzong of Tang Emperor of the Tang dynasty 805–820 Succeeded by Emperor Muzong of Tang v t e Emperors of the Tang dynasty Gaozu Taizong Gaozong Zhongzong Ruizong Wu Zetian Zhongzong ( 2nd reign ) Shang Ruizong ( 2nd reign ) Xuanzong Suzong Daizong Dezong Shunzong Xianzong Muzong Jingzong Wenzong Wuzong Xuānzong Yizong Xizong Zhaozong Ai 唐 Xia → Shang → Zhou → Qin → Han → 3 Kingdoms → Jìn / 16 Kingdoms → S."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d01", "start": 460, "end": 474, "text": "Dynasties / N."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d01", "start": 475, "end": 525, "text": "Dynasties → Sui → Tang → 5 Dynasties & 10 Kingdoms"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d02", "start": 0, "end": 57, "text": "was then grand empress dowager ) ( as Emperor Wenzong ) ."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d02", "start": 58, "end": 90, "text": "His name was changed to Li Ang ."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d02", "start": 91, "end": 446, "text": "[ 2 ] Reign [ edit ] Early Dahe / Taihe Era [ edit ] See also : Niu-Li factional strife It was said that at the start of Emperor Wenzong 's reign , he , having observed the wastefulness that his father Emperor Muzong and brother Emperor Jingzong displayed in their reigns , sought to conserve , govern diligently , and meet with the officials frequently ."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d02", "start": 447, "end": 504, "text": "The people thought that a peaceful time would be coming ."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d02", "start": 505, "end": 512, "text": "However"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 0, "end": 22, "text": "(光化公主) Ancestry [edit]"}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 23, "end": 55, "text": "Emperor Dezong of Tang (742–805)"}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 56, "end": 90, "text": "Emperor Shunzong of Tang (761–806)"}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 91, "end": 109, "text": "Empress Zhaode (d."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 110, "end": 114, "text": "786)"}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 115, "end": 170, "text": "Emperor Xianzong of Tang (778–820) - Father of Jingzong"}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 171, "end": 181, "text": "Wang Ziyan"}, "D4S8": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 182, "end": 210, "text": "Empress Zhuangxian (763–816)"}, "D4S9": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 211, "end": 243, "text": "Emperor Muzong of Tang (795–824)"}, "D4S10": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 244, "end": 262, "text": "Guo Ziyi (697–781)"}, "D4S11": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 263, "end": 279, "text": "Guo Ai (752–800)"}, "D4S12": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 280, "end": 299, "text": "Lady Wang (704–777)"}, "D4S13": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 300, "end": 317, "text": "Empress Yi'an (d."}, "D4S14": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 318, "end": 322, "text": "851)"}, "D4S15": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 323, "end": 356, "text": "Emperor Daizong of Tang (726–779)"}, "D4S16": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 357, "end": 378, "text": "Princess Qizhaoyi (d."}, "D4S17": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 379, "end": 383, "text": "810)"}, "D4S18": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 384, "end": 406, "text": "Lady Cui of Boling (d."}, "D4S19": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 407, "end": 411, "text": "757)"}, "D4S20": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 412, "end": 445, "text": "Emperor Wenzong of Tang (809–840)"}, "D4S21": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d03", "start": 446, "end": 464, "text": "Empress Zhenxian ("}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d04", "start": 0, "end": 224, "text": "Emperor Jingzong of Western Xia Emperor Jingzong of Western Xia (1003–1048), born Li Yuanhao (), or Tuoba Yuanhao (), was the first emperor of the Western Xia Empire located in northwestern China, reigning from 1038 to 1048."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d04", "start": 225, "end": 277, "text": "He was the eldest son of the Tangut ruler Li Deming."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d04", "start": 278, "end": 408, "text": "As a youth Jingzong was physically imposing yet also possessed a love of learning; he knew both the Tibetan and Chinese languages."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d04", "start": 409, "end": 530, "text": "Being a voracious reader, he was knowledgeable regarding matters of law and military strategy and also knew how to paint."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d04", "start": 531, "end": 582, "text": "After his father died in 1032, he became the leader"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d05", "start": 0, "end": 196, "text": "subsequently swore an oath, on his behalf, with Wang Xi, at their father Wang Shenzhi's tomb, formally entering a peace between the brothers, but privately, they remained suspicious of each other."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d05", "start": 197, "end": 407, "text": "Wang Xi subsequently build up Changle's defenses to defend a possible attack, and the conscription, as well as Wang Xi's encouragement for people to become Buddhist monks, led to some 11,000 men becoming monks."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d05", "start": 408, "end": 622, "text": "Meanwhile, Shi, who had previously cut off relations with Min due to the arrogance of Wang Jipeng (who wanted the two states to be treated equally, angering Shi), received Wang Xi's petition, and thereafter created"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d06", "start": 0, "end": 514, "text": ") Former Shu Wang Jian Wang Zongyan Later Shu Meng Zhixiang Meng Chang Jingnan Gao Jixing Gao Conghui Gao Baorong Gao Baoxu Gao Jichong Chu Ma Yin Ma Xisheng Ma Xifan Ma Xiguang Ma Xi ' e Ma Xichong Southern Han Liu Yan Liu Bin Liu Sheng Liu Chang Wu Yang Xingmi Yang Wo Yang Longyan Yang Pu Southern Tang Li Bian Li Jing Li Yu Min Wang Chao Wang Shenzhi Wang Yanhan Wang Yanjun Wang Jipeng Wang Yanxi Zhu Wenjin Yin Wang Yanzheng Wuyue Qian Liu Qian Yuanguan Qian Hongzuo Qian Hongzong Qian Chu Xia → Shang → Zhou"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d07", "start": 0, "end": 93, "text": "officers and female servants to submit a statement of oath and an incense censer to Wang Xi ."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d07", "start": 94, "end": 201, "text": "Subsequently , Wang Yanzheng 's emissaries and Wang Xi swore an oath at their father Wang Shenzhi 's tomb ."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d07", "start": 202, "end": 278, "text": "However , despite this oath , the suspicions between the brothers remained ."}, "D8S4": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d07", "start": 279, "end": 542, "text": "[ 7 ] In 941 , Wang Yanzheng fortified the defenses around Jian Prefecture , and he also requested Wang Xi make him the military governor of Weiwu Circuit ( 威武 , traditionally headquartered at Fu , but Wang Yanzheng thus was proposing to have its capital moved to"}, "D9S1": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0312-e56381ba0a0f:d08", "start": 0, "end": 321, "text": "this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Download as PDF Printable version In other projects 